{
  // Compiles `src/` with Node's types WITHHELD, so a bare Node global is a
  // compile error on every path — not only on the paths one fixture walks.
  //
  // `scripts/check-browser-bundle.mjs` fails on what esbuild cannot resolve: a
  // `node:` builtin, a bare specifier, a dynamic `require()`. It cannot fail on
  // `process.env.X`, `Buffer.from(...)`, `__dirname` or `global`, because those
  // are free identifiers rather than imports — esbuild leaves them in the
  // output untouched and reports nothing — and the vm smoke test in
  // `tests/browser-bundle.test.ts` sees only the code imm-001 executes. The
  // reason such a reference compiles at all is that `tsconfig.json` sets no
  // `types`, so every installed `@types/*` including `@types/node` is visible.
  // `types: []` is what does the work here; the DOM lib is what
  // `globalThis.crypto` and `TextEncoder` compile against without it.
  //
  // `lib` must be what `tsconfig.json` gets by default for target ES2022 —
  // `lib.es2022.full.d.ts`, which is `ES2022`, `DOM`, `DOM.Iterable` and
  // `DOM.AsyncIterable` (plus `ScriptHost` and `WebWorker.ImportScripts`,
  // which declare nothing `src/` could reach). Naming `lib` at all replaces
  // the default set, and without the iterable pair a
  // `[...new URLSearchParams(q)]` or a `for await (const chunk of body)` that
  // passes `npm run typecheck` and runs in every browser is reported here as
  // if it were a Node global.
  //
  // `allowJs` and the `.js` half of `include` are what let this see
  // `src/vendor/n3/n3.js` — 91 KB of shipped JavaScript that
  // `src/converter/to-rdf.ts` and `src/deserializer/n3-adapter.ts` import
  // statically, so it IS inlined into the browser bundle, and it is the file
  // a re-vendor is most likely to bring a `process.env.X` in through.
  // Without both, the compile opens no JavaScript at all and reports the gate
  // clean for never having looked. `checkJs` is what makes an opened `.js`
  // produce diagnostics; the base config's `declaration` is why `n3.d.ts`
  // still wins for anything that IMPORTS `./n3.js` — a declaration file
  // outranks its implementation, so the TypeScript half is unchanged by this.
  //
  // What comes back from a `.js` file is narrowed, and only there:
  // `scripts/lib/node-globals.mjs` keeps the unresolved-name diagnostics and
  // drops the rest. Untyped third-party JavaScript under `checkJs` produces
  // hundreds of inference findings — implicit `any` parameters, properties
  // absent from an inferred shape — none of them a Node global, all of them
  // about code nobody here wrote. A `.ts` file is still held to every
  // diagnostic it produces.
  //
  // Read by `scripts/lib/node-globals.mjs` through the TypeScript API, so the
  // same options judge a scratch source in a test and `src/` in the gate.
  // Never emits, never run by `npm run build`.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": [],
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable", "DOM.AsyncIterable"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.js"]
}
